<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masuria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masuria is a lake district in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, part of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. Its many glacial lakes include Śniardwy and Mamry. Once the land of the Old Prussian Galindians, it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and resettled from the 14th century, largely by Polish-speaking settlers from Mazovia, and it passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masuria.jpg" alt="Masuria" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: image/photo was taken by Wikipedia and Wikimedia Commons user Ludwig Schneider. I would appreciate being notified if ... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jezioro_Niegocin_w_okolicach_Gi%C5%BCycka_065.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Masuria occupies much of the Masurian Lake District, known for its 2,000 lakes, and covers some 10,000 square kilometres with about 500,000 inhabitants. It is bordered by Warmia, Powiśle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a> in the west, Mazovia in the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlachia</a> and the Suwałki Region in the east, and Lithuania Minor in the north. Its biggest city, Ełk, is often regarded as its capital.</p><p>The lakes were ground out of the land by glaciers during the Pleistocene ice age, and the terrain is hilly, with connecting lakes, rivers and streams. Forests account for about 30 per cent of the area: broadleaved in the north, pine and mixed in the south. The two largest lakes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Śniardwy and Mamry, lie here.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the 13th century the region was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>, a Baltic people, and part of it was known as Galindia, probably a deeply forested and lightly populated area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> in 1226, conquered it over more than 50 years; the original population was partly exterminated, particularly during the Prussian rebellion of 1261–1283. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> from Mazovia then began to settle in the south-east, followed by German, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Flemish and Danish colonists from the north-west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> was fought in western Masuria in 1410.</p><p>After the Order was secularised and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a> turned Lutheran in 1525, the area became Protestant under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Polish-speaking settlers from Mazovia filled the south, and Ełk and Węgorzewo became Polish printing centres. The region passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1701, and the name Masuria came into official use after the Prussian reforms after 1818. After the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1871 Polish lessons were removed from schools in 1872 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> policies spread.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the Yalta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> decisions the region passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945. Most of the remaining Germans were expelled, and Masurians who stayed faced a nationality verification by the new authorities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> from central Poland and the areas annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> resettled under Operation Vistula, moved in. Since 1999 Masuria and Warmia have formed the Warmian–Masurian Voivodeship, and the region hosts summer music festivals in Ostróda, Mrągowo, Giżycko and Ełk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masuria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy was home to German, Polish (mainly in <b>Masuria</b>) and Lithuanian (mainly in Lithuania Minor) speakers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Polish settlers moved into the Kulmerland and later into <b>Masuria</b>, while the surviving Prussians, converted to Christianity, became free tenants or dependent peasants; some Prussian nobles who had sided with the knights kept their status, but the Old Prussian language gradually gave way to German and died out…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy listed the Galindai in the 2nd century, and they are usually located in the lake country of southern Prussia, roughly modern <b>Masuria</b> in Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/galindians">Galindians</a></li></ul></section>
